# Resume builder

Everything here stays on this computer. The output goes in `{{ dir }}/`; their
original resume is never changed.

## When to offer it

Once `{{ config_dir_name }}/proof_bank.md` has at least three full entries (not
only raw notes) and `{{ config_dir_name }}/profile.md` says `Status: APPROVED`.
Offer it once, in a sentence; if they'd rather wait, don't bring it up again
unless they ask.

## Steps

1. **Consent.** In three lines: it reads their current resume, profile, story
   bank and `criteria.md`; it suggests changes section by section for them to
   accept or edit; it makes a new resume in `{{ dir }}/` and leaves the
   original alone. Ask whether to go ahead.
2. **Baseline.** Read their resume (the file used for `profile.md`), the
   approved `profile.md`, `proof_bank.md`, the Roles and "What matters most"
   sections of `criteria.md`, and `{{ me }}/phrasing_guide.md`. The guide shows
   patterns with invented examples; never reuse an example's facts or wording.
3. **Plan.** Tell them in three to five lines what you'd change and why, aimed
   at the roles in `criteria.md`. For example: the summary doesn't name what
   they actually build; several bullets start with "responsible for"; their
   strongest story-bank result isn't on the page yet.
4. **Section by section**: summary first, then each role from most recent,
   then skills, projects, education. For each, show **before → after**.
   - "After" uses only facts from their resume, profile, story bank or what
     they tell you now.
   - If a bullet needs a number they haven't given, ask ("Do you know roughly
     how many?"). If they don't know, write the result without one. Never
     fill one in.
   - Every role needs its real job title. If you don't know it, ask; never
     put a description ("first and only analyst") where the title goes.
   - If they prefer their own wording, keep it. It's their resume.
   - Coach the same way as `search-coach`: one question at a time, say why,
     no empty praise, no blunt verdicts.
   - US resumes leave out photos, date of birth, marital status and similar
     personal details. If theirs has them, suggest removing them and say why.
     Work authorization isn't on the resume unless they ask for it.
5. **Save** the approved content to `{{ dir }}/resume_content.md` (plain
   Markdown: header, summary, roles with bullets, skills, projects,
   education). Mark with `**…**` the few result numbers to emphasize. This file
   is the source of truth for every later change.
6. **Color.** Black text on white with one accent color for the name, the rule
   under it and the section headings. Offer these, or a color of their own:
   - Deep navy `#1F3A5F`: classic, safe anywhere
   - Jade `#1F6F5C`: calm and distinctive
   - Oxblood `#7A2631`: warm and confident
   - Slate teal `#2C5F6E`: modern and understated
   - Plum `#5A3470`: distinctive but still formal
   - Copper `#8C4A22`: warm, stands out in a pile

   A color of their own has to be dark enough to read as small text on white
   (contrast 4.5:1 or more). If it isn't, suggest a darker shade of the same color.
7. **Design step**, with their OK. Start a subagent (the Agent tool) so the
   page-building work stays out of this conversation, and give it these
   instructions (if subagents aren't available, do the same steps yourself):
   - Copy `{{ me }}/resume_template.html` to `{{ dir }}/resume.html` and set `--accent` to the chosen color.
   - Fill it from `{{ dir }}/resume_content.md` exactly: no rewording, no
     additions, no dropped facts. Bold only the numbers marked `**…**`.
     Remove sections they don't have.
   - Keep one column, real text, and no images, icons or tables, so
     applicant tracking systems can read it.
   - Fit one page: tighten spacing first. If it still doesn't fit, report
     which section overflows instead of cutting content.
   - Then run: `bash {{ me }}/render_pdf.sh {{ dir }}/resume.html {{ dir }}/<First>_<Last>_Resume.pdf`
8. **Check** the PDF: read it back and confirm it's one page, the text is
   selectable, and nothing is cut off. If `render_pdf.sh` found no browser,
   tell them to open `resume.html`, choose Print, then Save as PDF with
   headers and footers off.
9. **Hand over**: tell them where the PDF is, and ask them to read every line
   before they send it. They're the one vouching for it.

Later changes: edit `{{ dir }}/resume_content.md` with them, then repeat steps 7 and 8.

## Rules

- Never invent or inflate employers, titles, dates, numbers, tools or degrees.
- Never overwrite or delete their original resume.
- Nothing is uploaded anywhere: no resume sites, no online converters.
